<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf8">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="./spec.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/highlight.js/8.4/styles/github.min.css">
<script src="./spec.js"></script>
<pre class="metadata">
title: Amount
status: proposal
stage: 2
contributors: Ben Allen, Eemeli Aro, Jesse Alama
location: https://tc39.es/proposal-amount/
</pre>

<emu-intro id="sec-decimal-intro-">
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>This specification consists of three parts:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The specification of the <a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-amount/" title="Amount proposal (GitHub)">Amount proposal</a> and everything related to it, proposed to be added to ECMA&#8203;-262 in new sections;</li>
    <li>An amendment to Number.prototype.toExponential in ECMA&#8203;-262, which shares its rendering of exponential notation with Amount;</li>
    <li>A list of amendments to be made to ECMA-402.</li>
  </ul>
  <emu-note type="editor">
    <p>
      The changes proposed here are stacked on top of the <a href="https://tc39.es/proposal-intl-keep-trailing-zeros/">Keep Trailing Zeros</a> ECMA-402 proposal,
      and include calls to ECMA-402 Abstract Operations from ECMA&#8203;-262 algorithms.
      Where necessary, we intend to promote those semantics to ECMA&#8203;-262.
    </p>
  </emu-note>
</emu-intro>

<emu-clause id="sec-the-amount-object">
  <h1>The Amount Object</h1>
  <emu-intro id="sec-amount-intro">
    <h1>Introduction</h1>
    <p>An Amount is an object that wraps a numeric value—as a Number, BigInt, or String—together with an optional unit (e.g., mile, kilogram, EUR, JPY, USD-per-mile). One can intuitively understand an Amount as a value that, so to speak, knows what it is measuring.</p>
    <p>When precision options (such as fractionDigits or significantDigits) are applied, finite numeric values are stored as a <dfn id="decimal-digit-string">decimal digit string</dfn>: a String in |StrDecimalLiteral| form. Non-finite values are stored as the Number values *NaN*, *+&infin;*<sub>𝔽</sub>, or *-&infin;*<sub>𝔽</sub>. Otherwise, the original JavaScript value type (Number, BigInt, or String) is retained.</p>
    <p>An Amount whose unit is a <emu-xref href="#amount-sequence-unit">sequence unit</emu-xref>, such as foot-and-inch, holds one numeric value per <emu-xref href="#amount-unit-components">component</emu-xref>. Its [[AmountValue]] is a List with one element per component; every element but the last is an integer. The last element may carry a fractional part and precision information, exactly as the value of a single-unit Amount does.</p>
    <p>Unit conversion computes with Number arithmetic. Its result is therefore a Number unless precision options are applied to it, whatever the value type of the Amount being converted.</p>
    <p>Rounding a mathematical value is an important part of this spec. When we say <dfn id="amount-rounding-mode">rounding mode</dfn> in this specification we simply refer to <emu-xref href="#table-intl-rounding-modes">ECMA-402's definition</emu-xref>.</p>
  </emu-intro>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount-unit-identifiers">
    <h1>Unit Identifiers</h1>
    <p>The unit of an Amount is given as a <dfn id="amount-unit-identifier">unit identifier</dfn>: a String that matches the |UnitIdentifier| goal symbol of the following grammar.</p>
    <p>A unit identifier that matches |SequenceUnitIdentifier| is a <dfn id="amount-sequence-unit" variants="sequence units">sequence unit</dfn>. Its <dfn id="amount-unit-components" variants="component">components</dfn> are the substrings separated by *"-and-"*, in order from the largest to the smallest unit of measurement. A unit identifier that matches |SingleUnitIdentifier| is a <dfn id="amount-single-unit" variants="single units">single unit</dfn> and is its own sole component.</p>
    <h2>Syntax</h2>
    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      UnitIdentifier :::
        SingleUnitIdentifier
        SequenceUnitIdentifier

      SequenceUnitIdentifier :::
        SingleUnitIdentifier `-and-` SingleUnitIdentifier
        SequenceUnitIdentifier `-and-` SingleUnitIdentifier

      SingleUnitIdentifier :::
        UnitIdentifierPart
        SingleUnitIdentifier `-` UnitIdentifierPart

      UnitIdentifierPart :::
        UnitIdentifierPartChars but not `and`

      UnitIdentifierPartChars :::
        IdentifierPartChar
        UnitIdentifierPartChars IdentifierPartChar
    </emu-grammar>
    <emu-note>
      <p>This grammar is inspired by the syntax of <a href="https://unicode.org/reports/tr35/tr35-general.html#Unit_Identifiers">Unicode Technical Standard #35 Part 2 General, Unit Identifiers</a>, but is not identical to it: each hyphen-separated segment admits any |IdentifierPartChar|, aligning the accepted code points with ECMAScript identifier syntax. The segment `and` is reserved for joining the components of a sequence unit, so it cannot appear as a segment of a single unit, and splitting a unit identifier at every occurrence of *"-and-"* yields exactly its components.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount-finite-numeric-literals">
    <h1>Finite Numeric Literals</h1>
    <p>A String value given to the Amount constructor must denote a finite numeric value, which is defined as matching the |StrFiniteNumericLiteral| goal symbol of the following grammar.</p>
    <h2>Syntax</h2>
    <emu-grammar type="definition">
      StrFiniteNumericLiteral :::
        StrNumericLiteral but not one of `Infinity` or `+Infinity` or `-Infinity`
    </emu-grammar>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount-abstract-operations">
    <h1>Abstract Operations</h1>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-getunitcomponents" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetUnitComponents (
            _unit_: a String,
          ): a List of Strings
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the components of _unit_, which must be a unit identifier. The result has one element if _unit_ is a single unit, and two or more elements if _unit_ is a sequence unit.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Assert: ParseText(_unit_, |UnitIdentifier|) is a Parse Node.
          1. Return StringSplitToList(_unit_, *"-and-"*).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <!-- Copied and modified from ECMA-402 GetOption -->
      <!-- The difference: -->
      <!-- Added support for ~number~ as an expected data type -->

      <emu-clause id="sec-getoption" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetOption (
            _options_: an Object,
            _property_: a property key,
            _type_: ~boolean~, ~string~ or ~number~,
            _values_: ~empty~ or a List of ECMAScript language values,
            _default_: ~required~ or an ECMAScript language value,
          ): either a normal completion containing an ECMAScript language value or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It extracts the value of the specified property of _options_, converts it to the required _type_, checks whether it is allowed by _values_ if _values_ is not ~empty~, and substitutes _default_ if the value is *undefined*.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _value_ be ? Get(_options_, _property_).
          1. If _value_ is *undefined*, then
            1. If _default_ is ~required~, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Return _default_.
          1. If _type_ is ~boolean~, then
            1. Set _value_ to ToBoolean(_value_).
          1. Else if _type_ is ~number~, then
            1. Set _value_ to ? ToNumber(_value_).
          1. Else,
            1. Assert: _type_ is ~string~.
            1. Set _value_ to ? ToString(_value_).
          1. If _values_ is not ~empty~ and _values_ does not contain _value_, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Return _value_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-options-record">
        <h1>Amount Options Record</h1>
        <p>
          An <dfn variants="Amount Options Records">Amount Options Record</dfn> is a Record holding the resolved values of the options
          accepted by the Amount constructor and by <emu-xref href="#sec-amount.prototype.convertto">Amount.prototype.convertTo</emu-xref>.
          It has fields [[FractionDigits]] (a non-negative integer or *undefined*), [[RoundingMode]] (a rounding mode),
          [[SignificantDigits]] (a positive integer or *undefined*), [[Unit]] (a String or *undefined*),
          and [[ExtendedOptions]] (*undefined*, or a value as specified in the ECMA-402 Internationalization API).
        </p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The [[ExtendedOptions]] field is reserved for options that are meaningful only in an implementation that includes the ECMA-402 Internationalization API. It is always *undefined* in an implementation that does not include ECMA-402.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-getamountoptions" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>GetAmountOptions (
          _opts_: an ECMAScript language value,
          optional _conversionSource_: a Conversion Source Record,
        ): either a normal completion containing an Amount Options Record or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It validates the given _opts_ and returns their resolved values. A String _opts_ is treated as a shorthand for specifying only a unit. If _conversionSource_ is present, the [[Unit]] field of the returned Record is the unit that the quantity described by _conversionSource_ is to be converted to, which the options may select without naming it explicitly.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _resolvedOpts_ be the Amount Options Record { [[FractionDigits]]: *undefined*, [[RoundingMode]]: *"halfEven"*, [[SignificantDigits]]: *undefined*, [[Unit]]: *undefined*, [[ExtendedOptions]]: *undefined* }.
          1. If _opts_ is a String, then
            1. If ParseText(_opts_, |UnitIdentifier|) is not a Parse Node, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Set _resolvedOpts_.[[Unit]] to _opts_.
            1. Return _resolvedOpts_.
          1. Set _opts_ to ? GetOptionsObject(_opts_).
          1. Perform ? SetAmountOptions(_resolvedOpts_, _opts_).
          1. Let _fractionDigits_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[FractionDigits]].
          1. Let _significantDigits_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[SignificantDigits]].
          1. If _fractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, then
            1. If _significantDigits_ is not *undefined*, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. If _fractionDigits_ is not an integral Number, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Set _fractionDigits_ to ℝ(_fractionDigits_).
            1. If _fractionDigits_ is not in the inclusive interval from 0 to 100, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Set _resolvedOpts_.[[FractionDigits]] to _fractionDigits_.
          1. Else if _significantDigits_ is not *undefined*, then
            1. If _significantDigits_ is not an integral Number, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Set _significantDigits_ to ℝ(_significantDigits_).
            1. If _significantDigits_ is not in the inclusive interval from 1 to 21, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Set _resolvedOpts_.[[SignificantDigits]] to _significantDigits_.
          1. If _conversionSource_ is present, set _resolvedOpts_.[[Unit]] to ? SelectTargetUnit(_conversionSource_, _resolvedOpts_).
          1. Return _resolvedOpts_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-setamountoptions" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SetAmountOptions (
            _target_: an Amount Options Record,
            _source_: an Object,
          ): either a normal completion containing ~unused~ or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It sets the fields of _target_ from the properties of _source_, which are read in alphabetical order. Validation that depends on more than one option is left to the caller.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
          An ECMAScript implementation that includes the ECMA-402 Internationalization API
          must implement this abstract operation as specified in the ECMA-402 specification.
          If an ECMAScript implementation does not include the ECMA-402 API,
          SetAmountOptions performs the following steps when called:
        </p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Set _target_.[[FractionDigits]] to ? GetOption(_source_, *"fractionDigits"*, ~number~, ~empty~, *undefined*).
          1. Set _target_.[[RoundingMode]] to ? GetOption(_source_, *"roundingMode"*, ~string~, « *"ceil"*, *"floor"*, *"expand"*, *"trunc"*, *"halfCeil"*, *"halfFloor"*, *"halfExpand"*, *"halfTrunc"*, *"halfEven"* », *"halfEven"*).
          1. Set _target_.[[SignificantDigits]] to ? GetOption(_source_, *"significantDigits"*, ~number~, ~empty~, *undefined*).
          1. Let _unit_ be ? GetOption(_source_, *"unit"*, ~string~, ~empty~, *undefined*).
          1. If _unit_ is not *undefined*, then
            1. If ParseText(_unit_, |UnitIdentifier|) is not a Parse Node, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Set _target_.[[Unit]] to _unit_.
          1. Return ~unused~.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-selecttargetunit" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SelectTargetUnit (
            _conversionSource_: a Conversion Source Record,
            _resolvedOpts_: an Amount Options Record,
          ): either a normal completion containing a String or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It determines the unit identifier to which the quantity described by _conversionSource_ is to be converted.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p>
          An ECMAScript implementation that includes the ECMA-402 Internationalization API
          must implement this abstract operation as specified in the ECMA-402 specification.
          If an ECMAScript implementation does not include the ECMA-402 API,
          SelectTargetUnit performs the following steps when called:
        </p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _unit_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[Unit]].
          1. If _unit_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return _unit_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-getamountnumericvalue" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetAmountNumericValue (
            _v_: a Number, a BigInt, or a String,
          ): a Number
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the Number that most closely approximates the stored Amount value or sequence entry _v_. The sign of a negative zero is preserved.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _v_ is a Number, return _v_.
          1. If _v_ is a BigInt, return 𝔽(ℝ(_v_)).
          1. Assert: _v_ is a String.
          1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(_v_, |StringNumericLiteral|).
          1. Assert: _parsed_ is a Parse Node.
          1. Let _intlMV_ be the StringIntlMV of _parsed_.
          1. If _intlMV_.[[Value]] is ~negative-zero~, return *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>.
          1. Return 𝔽(_intlMV_.[[Value]]).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-roundtofractiondigits" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RoundToFractionDigits (
            _mv_: a mathematical value or ~negative-zero~,
            _fractionDigits_: a non-negative integer,
            _roundingMode_: a rounding mode,
          ): a String
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It rounds _mv_ to _fractionDigits_ fraction digits using _roundingMode_ and returns the result as a decimal digit string in exponential notation with as many significant digits as the rounding produced. The sign of _mv_, including that of negative zero, is preserved.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _formatter_ be CreateFormatterObject(_roundingMode_, _fractionDigits_, _fractionDigits_, *undefined*, *undefined*, *undefined*).
          1. Let _fmt_ be FormatNumericToString(_formatter_, _mv_, 0, 0).
          1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(_fmt_.[[FormattedString]], |StringNumericLiteral|).
          1. Assert: _parsed_ is a Parse Node.
          1. Let _fmtIntlMV_ be the StringIntlMV of _parsed_.
          1. Return RenderInExponentialNotation(_fmt_.[[RoundedNumber]], _fmtIntlMV_.[[StringDigitCount]]).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>[[FormattedString]] carries no sign, so it determines only the number of digits; the value comes from [[RoundedNumber]].</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-roundtosignificantdigits" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RoundToSignificantDigits (
            _mv_: a mathematical value or ~negative-zero~,
            _significantDigits_: a positive integer,
            _roundingMode_: a rounding mode,
          ): a String
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It rounds _mv_ to _significantDigits_ significant digits using _roundingMode_ and returns the result as a decimal digit string in exponential notation with _significantDigits_ significant digits. The sign of _mv_, including that of negative zero, is preserved.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _formatter_ be CreateFormatterObject(_roundingMode_, *undefined*, *undefined*, _significantDigits_, _significantDigits_, *undefined*).
          1. Let _fmt_ be FormatNumericToString(_formatter_, _mv_, 0, 0).
          1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(_fmt_.[[FormattedString]], |StringNumericLiteral|).
          1. Assert: _parsed_ is a Parse Node.
          1. Let _fmtIntlMV_ be the StringIntlMV of _parsed_.
          1. Return RenderInExponentialNotation(_fmt_.[[RoundedNumber]], _fmtIntlMV_.[[StringDigitCount]]).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-roundamountvalue" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RoundAmountValue (
            _value_: a Number, a BigInt, or an Intl MV Record,
            _resolvedOpts_: an Amount Options Record,
          ): a Number, a BigInt, or a String
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the value to store in an Amount for the input _value_ under the precision options of _resolvedOpts_. A finite value to which precision options apply, and any value that came from a String, is returned as a decimal digit string in exponential notation; otherwise _value_ is returned unchanged.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _fractionDigits_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[FractionDigits]].
          1. Let _significantDigits_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[SignificantDigits]].
          1. If _fractionDigits_ is not *undefined* or _significantDigits_ is not *undefined*, then
            1. If _value_ is a finite Number or _value_ is not a Number, then
              1. If _value_ is an Intl MV Record, let _mv_ be _value_.[[Value]].
              1. Else if _value_ is *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, let _mv_ be ~negative-zero~.
              1. Else, let _mv_ be ℝ(_value_).
              1. Let _roundingMode_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[RoundingMode]].
              1. If _fractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, return RoundToFractionDigits(_mv_, _fractionDigits_, _roundingMode_).
              1. Return RoundToSignificantDigits(_mv_, _significantDigits_, _roundingMode_).
            1. Return _value_.
          1. If _value_ is an Intl MV Record, then
            1. Let _mv_ be _value_.[[Value]].
            1. If _value_.[[StringDigitCount]] ≥ 1, then
              1. Let _digitCount_ be _value_.[[StringDigitCount]].
            1. Else,
              1. Assert: _mv_ is an integer.
              1. Let _digitCount_ be the count of digits in the decimal representation of _mv_.
            1. Return RenderInExponentialNotation(_mv_, _digitCount_).
          1. Return _value_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-readsequenceentries" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          ReadSequenceEntries (
            _value_: an Object,
            _components_: a List of Strings,
          ): either a normal completion containing a List of either Numbers, BigInts, or Intl MV Records or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It reads one entry per element of _components_ from _value_, which is either an Array of the same length or an object with a property named after each component, and validates the entries: every entry but the last must be an integer, and no entry may be negative while another is positive.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _n_ be the number of elements in _components_.
          1. Let _raw_ be a new empty List.
          1. If ? IsArray(_value_) is *true*, then
            1. Let _len_ be ? LengthOfArrayLike(_value_).
            1. If _len_ ≠ _n_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _k_ be 0.
            1. Repeat, while _k_ &lt; _n_,
              1. Let _element_ be ? Get(_value_, ! ToString(𝔽(_k_))).
              1. Append _element_ to _raw_.
              1. Set _k_ to _k_ + 1.
          1. Else,
            1. For each element _component_ of _components_, do
              1. Let _element_ be ? Get(_value_, _component_).
              1. If _element_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
              1. Append _element_ to _raw_.
          1. Let _entries_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _hasNegative_ be *false*.
          1. Let _hasPositive_ be *false*.
          1. Let _k_ be 0.
          1. For each element _element_ of _raw_, do
            1. If _k_ = _n_ - 1, let _isLast_ be *true*; else let _isLast_ be *false*.
            1. If _element_ is a String, then
              1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(_element_, |StrFiniteNumericLiteral|).
              1. If _parsed_ is not a Parse Node, throw a *RangeError* exception.
              1. Let _entry_ be the StringIntlMV of _parsed_.
              1. Let _mv_ be _entry_.[[Value]].
              1. If _mv_ is not ~negative-zero~, then
                1. If _mv_ &lt; 0, set _hasNegative_ to *true*.
                1. If _mv_ &gt; 0, set _hasPositive_ to *true*.
                1. If _isLast_ is *false* and _mv_ is not an integer, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Else if _element_ is a Number, then
              1. Let _entry_ be _element_.
              1. If _entry_ &lt; *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, set _hasNegative_ to *true*.
              1. If _entry_ &gt; *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, set _hasPositive_ to *true*.
              1. If _isLast_ is *false* and _entry_ is not an integral Number, throw a *RangeError* exception.
            1. Else if _element_ is a BigInt, then
              1. Let _entry_ be _element_.
              1. If ℝ(_entry_) &lt; 0, set _hasNegative_ to *true*.
              1. If ℝ(_entry_) &gt; 0, set _hasPositive_ to *true*.
            1. Else,
              1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Append _entry_ to _entries_.
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ + 1.
          1. If _hasNegative_ is *true* and _hasPositive_ is *true*, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Return _entries_.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>*NaN* and the infinities are not integral Numbers, so they are rejected in every position but the last. Extra properties of an object _value_ are ignored, matching the handling of sequence unit values by Intl.NumberFormat.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-createformatterobject" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>CreateFormatterObject (
          _roundingMode_: a String,
          _minFractionDigits_: a non-negative integer or *undefined*,
          _maxFractionDigits_: a non-negative integer or *undefined*,
          _minSignificantDigits_: a positive integer or *undefined*,
          _maxSignificantDigits_: a positive integer or *undefined*,
          _roundingPriority_: a String or *undefined*
        ): an Object
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It creates an object with the internal slots required by <emu-xref href="#sec-formatnumerictostring">FormatNumericToString</emu-xref>, configured according to the given rounding and digit options.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _formatter_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(*null*, « [[MinimumFractionDigits]], [[MinimumIntegerDigits]], [[MinimumSignificantDigits]], [[MaximumFractionDigits]], [[MaximumSignificantDigits]], [[RoundingIncrement]], [[RoundingMode]], [[RoundingType]], [[TrailingZeroDisplay]] »).
          1. Set _formatter_.[[RoundingMode]] to _roundingMode_.
          1. Set _formatter_.[[MinimumIntegerDigits]] to 1.
          1. Set _formatter_.[[RoundingIncrement]] to 1.
          1. Set _formatter_.[[TrailingZeroDisplay]] to *"auto"*.
          1. If _minSignificantDigits_ is not *undefined* or _maxSignificantDigits_ is not *undefined*, let _hasSD_ be *true*; else let _hasSD_ be *false*.
          1. If _minFractionDigits_ is not *undefined* or _maxFractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, let _hasFD_ be *true*; else let _hasFD_ be *false*.
          1. If _hasSD_ is *true*, then
            1. If _minSignificantDigits_ is not *undefined*, let _resolvedMinSD_ be _minSignificantDigits_; else let _resolvedMinSD_ be 1.
            1. If _maxSignificantDigits_ is not *undefined*, let _resolvedMaxSD_ be _maxSignificantDigits_; else let _resolvedMaxSD_ be 21.
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MinimumSignificantDigits]] to _resolvedMinSD_.
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MaximumSignificantDigits]] to _resolvedMaxSD_.
          1. Else,
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MinimumSignificantDigits]] to 1.
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MaximumSignificantDigits]] to 21.
          1. If _hasFD_ is *true*, then
            1. If _minFractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, let _resolvedMinFD_ be _minFractionDigits_; else let _resolvedMinFD_ be 0.
            1. If _maxFractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, let _resolvedMaxFD_ be _maxFractionDigits_; else let _resolvedMaxFD_ be 100.
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MinimumFractionDigits]] to _resolvedMinFD_.
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MaximumFractionDigits]] to _resolvedMaxFD_.
          1. Else,
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MinimumFractionDigits]] to 0.
            1. Set _formatter_.[[MaximumFractionDigits]] to 100.
          1. If _hasSD_ is *true* and _hasFD_ is *true*, then
            1. If _roundingPriority_ is *"morePrecision"*, set _formatter_.[[RoundingType]] to ~more-precision~; else set _formatter_.[[RoundingType]] to ~less-precision~.
          1. Else if _hasSD_ is *true*, then
            1. Set _formatter_.[[RoundingType]] to ~significant-digits~.
          1. Else,
            1. Set _formatter_.[[RoundingType]] to ~fraction-digits~.
          1. Return _formatter_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-unit-conversion-data">
        <h1>Unit Conversion Data</h1>
        <p>Unit conversion data is derived from CLDR file <a href="https://github.com/unicode-org/cldr/blob/main/common/supplemental/units.xml"><code>supplemental/units.xml</code></a>. As described in <a href="https://unicode.org/reports/tr35/tr35-info.html#conversion-data">Unicode Technical Standard #35 Part 6 Supplemental, Conversion Data</a>, each <code>&lt;convertUnit&gt;</code> element defines how to convert a <code>source</code> unit into a compatible <code>baseUnit</code>. An ECMAScript implementation must ignore all <code>special</code> conversions and support all conversions based on <code>factor</code> and/or <code>offset</code>, interpreting the value for each as an arithmetic expression with mathematical value operands (noting the respective defaults of 1 and 0 and the implicit presence of an identity mapping for each unit identified as the value of a <code>baseUnit</code>).</p>
        <p>Two units are convertible if and only if they share the same <code>baseUnit</code> value in CLDR. A unit that appears as a <code>baseUnit</code> value has an implicit identity conversion (<code>factor</code> 1, <code>offset</code> 0).</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>In <code>factor</code> and <code>offset</code> expressions, <code>*</code> multiplication binds more tightly than <code>/</code> division, and constants defined by <code>&lt;unitConstant&gt;</code> elements are valid operands.</p>
          <p>For example:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>&lt;convertUnit source='celsius' baseUnit='kelvin' offset='273.15' systems="si metric"/&gt;</code> specifies that conversion from unit "celsius" to unit "kelvin" requires addition of 273.15.</li>
            <li><code>&lt;convertUnit source='fahrenheit' baseUnit='kelvin' factor='5/9' offset='2298.35/9' systems="ussystem uksystem"/&gt;</code> specifies that conversion from unit "fahrenheit" to unit "kelvin" requires multiplication by (5 / 9) followed by addition of (2298.35 / 9).</li>
            <li><code>&lt;convertUnit source='radian' baseUnit='revolution' factor='1/2*PI' systems="si metric"/&gt;</code> specifies that conversion from unit "radian" to unit "revolution" requires multiplication by (1 / (2 × <code>PI</code>)), which references <code>&lt;unitConstant constant="PI" value="411557987 / 131002976" status='approximate'/&gt;</code> and is thus equivalent to multiplication by (1 / (2 × (411557987 / 131002976))) = (131002976 / 823115974).</li>
            <li><code>&lt;convertUnit source='solar-mass' baseUnit='kilogram' factor='1.98847E+30' systems="astronomical"/&gt;</code> specifies that conversion from unit "solar-mass" to unit "kilogram" requires multiplication by (1.98847 × 10<sup>30</sup>).</li>
          </ul>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-renderamountvalue" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RenderAmountValue (
            _v_: a Number, a BigInt, or a String,
          ): a String
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It renders a stored Amount value in canonical exponential notation, or as *"NaN"*, *"Infinity"*, or *"-Infinity"* for non-finite Numbers. A finite Number is rendered with the fewest significant digits that identify it, as Number.prototype.toExponential does when called without an argument, except that negative zero keeps its sign.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _v_ is a String, return _v_.
          1. If _v_ is a Number, then
            1. If _v_ is *NaN*, return *"NaN"*.
            1. If _v_ is *+&infin;*<sub>𝔽</sub>, return *"Infinity"*.
            1. If _v_ is *-&infin;*<sub>𝔽</sub>, return *"-Infinity"*.
            1. If _v_ is *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, return *"-0e+0"*.
            1. If _v_ is *+0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, return *"0e+0"*.
            1. If _v_ &lt; *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, return the string-concatenation of *"-"* and RenderAmountValue(-_v_).
            1. Let _n_, _k_, and _s_ be integers such that _k_ ≥ 1, 10<sup>_k_ - 1</sup> ≤ _s_ &lt; 10<sup>_k_</sup>, 𝔽(_s_ × 10<sup>_n_ - _k_</sup>) is _v_, and _k_ is as small as possible. If there are multiple possibilities for _s_, choose the value of _s_ for which _s_ × 10<sup>_n_ - _k_</sup> is closest in value to ℝ(_v_). If there are two such possible values of _s_, choose the one that is even.
            1. Return RenderInExponentialNotation(_s_ × 10<sup>_n_ - _k_</sup>, _k_).
          1. Assert: _v_ is a BigInt.
          1. Let _mv_ be ℝ(_v_).
          1. Let _digitCount_ be the number of digits in the decimal representation of abs(_mv_).
          1. Return RenderInExponentialNotation(_mv_, _digitCount_).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-rendersequenceentries" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          RenderSequenceEntries (
            _entries_: a List of either Numbers, BigInts, or Strings,
            _components_: a List of Strings,
          ): a String
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It renders the stored entries of a sequence-unit Amount, each followed by its component unit and separated by *", "*. Every entry but the last is an integer and is rendered as a plain decimal integer; the last is rendered as the value of a single-unit Amount is.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _n_ be the number of elements in _entries_.
          1. Assert: The number of elements in _components_ is _n_.
          1. Let _result_ be the empty String.
          1. Let _k_ be 0.
          1. Repeat, while _k_ &lt; _n_,
            1. Let _entry_ be _entries_[_k_].
            1. If _k_ = _n_ - 1, then
              1. Let _entryStr_ be RenderAmountValue(_entry_).
            1. Else if _entry_ is a String, then
              1. Let _entryStr_ be _entry_.
            1. Else if _entry_ is a BigInt, then
              1. Let _entryStr_ be BigInt::toString(_entry_, 10).
            1. Else if _entry_ is *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, then
              1. Let _entryStr_ be *"-0"*.
            1. Else,
              1. Assert: _entry_ is an integral Number.
              1. Let _entryStr_ be the String value consisting of the decimal representation of ℝ(_entry_) with no leading zeros.
            1. If _k_ &gt; 0, set _result_ to the string-concatenation of _result_ and *", "*.
            1. Set _result_ to the string-concatenation of _result_, _entryStr_, *" "*, and _components_[_k_].
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ + 1.
          1. Return _result_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-getunitconversionfactor" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>GetUnitConversionFactor (
          _unit_: a String
        ): either a normal completion containing a Record with fields [[BaseUnit]] (a String), [[Factor]] (a mathematical value), and [[Offset]] (a mathematical value) or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the conversion data for converting _unit_ to its base unit.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If _unit_ is the <code>source</code> of a <code>&lt;convertUnit&gt;</code> element in the <emu-xref href="#sec-amount-unit-conversion-data">unit conversion data</emu-xref>, then
            1. Let _element_ be that <code>&lt;convertUnit&gt;</code> element.
            1. If _element_ has an attribute <code>special</code>, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _baseUnit_ be the <code>baseUnit</code> of _element_.
            1. Let _factor_ be the mathematical value of the <code>factor</code> attribute of _element_ if present, or 1 otherwise.
            1. Let _offset_ be the mathematical value of the <code>offset</code> attribute of _element_ if present, or 0 otherwise.
          1. Else if _unit_ is a <code>baseUnit</code> value in the <emu-xref href="#sec-amount-unit-conversion-data">unit conversion data</emu-xref>, then
            1. Let _baseUnit_ be _unit_.
            1. Let _factor_ be 1.
            1. Let _offset_ be 0.
          1. Else,
            1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Return the Record { [[BaseUnit]]: _baseUnit_, [[Factor]]: _factor_, [[Offset]]: _offset_ }.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The formula for converting a value in _unit_ to its base unit is: <i>baseValue</i> = <i>value</i> × [[Factor]] + [[Offset]].</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-convertunitvalue" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>ConvertUnitValue (
          _value_: a Number,
          _sourceUnit_: a String,
          _targetUnit_: a String
        ): either a normal completion containing a Number or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It converts _value_ from _sourceUnit_ to _targetUnit_ using Number arithmetic.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. If SameValue(_sourceUnit_, _targetUnit_) is *true*, return _value_.
          1. Let _sourceConv_ be ? GetUnitConversionFactor(_sourceUnit_).
          1. Let _targetConv_ be ? GetUnitConversionFactor(_targetUnit_).
          1. If _sourceConv_.[[BaseUnit]] is not _targetConv_.[[BaseUnit]], throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _value_ is *NaN*, return *NaN*.
          1. Let _sourceFactor_ be _sourceConv_.[[Factor]].
          1. Let _sourceOffset_ be _sourceConv_.[[Offset]].
          1. Let _targetFactor_ be _targetConv_.[[Factor]].
          1. Let _targetOffset_ be _targetConv_.[[Offset]].
          1. If _sourceOffset_ is _targetOffset_, then
            1. NOTE: This preserves a _value_ of *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>.
            1. Return _value_ × 𝔽(_sourceFactor_ / _targetFactor_).
          1. Return _value_ × 𝔽(_sourceFactor_ / _targetFactor_) + 𝔽((_sourceOffset_ - _targetOffset_) / _targetFactor_).
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>The factor term _sourceFactor_ / _targetFactor_ and the offset term (_sourceOffset_ − _targetOffset_) / _targetFactor_ are computed as mathematical values, and then converted to Number values for the multiplication and addition. For non-offset conversions (the vast majority), the offset term is 0 and addition is skipped in order to preserve an input _value_ of *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-conversion-source-record">
        <h1>Conversion Source Record</h1>
        <p>
          A <dfn variants="Conversion Source Records">Conversion Source Record</dfn> is a Record describing the quantity that a unit conversion starts from.
          It has fields [[Value]] (a Number) and [[Unit]] (a String), where [[Unit]] is a <emu-xref href="#amount-single-unit">single unit</emu-xref> and [[Value]] is the quantity of that unit.
          For a single-unit Amount these are the Amount's own value and unit. For a sequence-unit Amount, [[Unit]] is the last (smallest) component and [[Value]] is the total of all entries expressed in that component.
        </p>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-getsequenceunitdata" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetSequenceUnitData (
            _components_: a List of Strings,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Record with fields [[BaseUnit]] (a String) and [[Multipliers]] (a List of integers) or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It checks that _components_, the components of a sequence unit, are units that convert to the same base unit without an offset, in decreasing order of magnitude, with each unit an integer multiple of the next. [[Multipliers]][_k_] is the number of units of _components_[_k_ + 1] in one unit of _components_[_k_].</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _n_ be the number of elements in _components_.
          1. Assert: _n_ ≥ 2.
          1. Let _first_ be ? GetUnitConversionFactor(_components_[0]).
          1. If _first_.[[Offset]] ≠ 0, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _baseUnit_ be _first_.[[BaseUnit]].
          1. Let _previousFactor_ be _first_.[[Factor]].
          1. Let _multipliers_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _k_ be 1.
          1. Repeat, while _k_ &lt; _n_,
            1. Let _conv_ be ? GetUnitConversionFactor(_components_[_k_]).
            1. If _conv_.[[BaseUnit]] is not _baseUnit_, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. If _conv_.[[Offset]] ≠ 0, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Let _ratio_ be _previousFactor_ / _conv_.[[Factor]].
            1. If _ratio_ is not an integer or _ratio_ &lt; 2, throw a *TypeError* exception.
            1. Append _ratio_ to _multipliers_.
            1. Set _previousFactor_ to _conv_.[[Factor]].
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ + 1.
          1. Return the Record { [[BaseUnit]]: _baseUnit_, [[Multipliers]]: _multipliers_ }.
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>Conversion factors are mathematical values taken from CLDR, so the ratio of two factors is exact. Whether a sequence unit can also be formatted by Intl.NumberFormat is decided separately by ECMA-402, which admits only a fixed list of sequences.</p>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-sequencetotal" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SequenceTotal (
            _entries_: a List of either Numbers, BigInts, or Strings,
            _multipliers_: a List of integers,
          ): a Number
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns the Number that most closely approximates the total of _entries_, the stored entries of a sequence-unit Amount, expressed in the last (smallest) component unit. The total is computed exactly and rounded once. If the total is zero, the sign of the last entry is preserved.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _n_ be the number of elements in _entries_.
          1. Assert: The number of elements in _multipliers_ is _n_ - 1.
          1. Let _last_ be _entries_[_n_ - 1].
          1. If _last_ is a Number and _last_ is not finite, return _last_.
          1. Let _sum_ be 0.
          1. Let _scale_ be 1.
          1. Let _k_ be _n_ - 1.
          1. Repeat, while _k_ ≥ 0,
            1. Let _entry_ be _entries_[_k_].
            1. If _entry_ is a String, then
              1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(_entry_, |StringNumericLiteral|).
              1. Assert: _parsed_ is a Parse Node.
              1. Let _intlMV_ be the StringIntlMV of _parsed_.
              1. Let _mv_ be _intlMV_.[[Value]].
            1. Else,
              1. Let _mv_ be ℝ(_entry_).
            1. If _mv_ is ~negative-zero~, set _mv_ to 0.
            1. If _k_ &lt; _n_ - 1, then
              1. Assert: _mv_ is an integer.
            1. Set _sum_ to _sum_ + _mv_ × _scale_.
            1. If _k_ &gt; 0, set _scale_ to _scale_ × _multipliers_[_k_ - 1].
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ - 1.
          1. If _sum_ = 0, return GetAmountNumericValue(_last_).
          1. Return 𝔽(_sum_).
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-getamountconversionsource" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          GetAmountConversionSource (
            _amount_: an Amount,
          ): either a normal completion containing a Conversion Source Record or a throw completion
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It expresses _amount_ as a Number in a single unit so that it can be converted. It throws a *TypeError* exception if the unit of _amount_ has no conversion data, or is a sequence unit whose components are not convertible with each other.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _unit_ be _amount_.[[Unit]].
          1. Assert: _unit_ is a String.
          1. Let _components_ be GetUnitComponents(_unit_).
          1. Let _v_ be _amount_.[[AmountValue]].
          1. If the number of elements in _components_ is 1, then
            1. Assert: _v_ is not a List.
            1. Perform ? GetUnitConversionFactor(_unit_).
            1. Return the Conversion Source Record { [[Value]]: GetAmountNumericValue(_v_), [[Unit]]: _unit_ }.
          1. Assert: _v_ is a List.
          1. Let _data_ be ? GetSequenceUnitData(_components_).
          1. Let _total_ be SequenceTotal(_v_, _data_.[[Multipliers]]).
          1. Return the Conversion Source Record { [[Value]]: _total_, [[Unit]]: the last element of _components_ }.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-amount-splitintosequence" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>
          SplitIntoSequence (
            _value_: a finite Number,
            _multipliers_: a List of integers,
            _resolvedOpts_: an Amount Options Record,
          ): a List of either Numbers or Strings
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It expresses _value_, a quantity of the last (smallest) component unit of a sequence unit, as the entries of that sequence unit. The value is first rounded according to the precision options of _resolvedOpts_, so that rounding can carry into a larger component, and then divided by the multipliers from the smallest unit up, truncating toward zero so that every entry has the sign of _value_. A zero entry of a negative _value_ is negative zero. Every entry but the last is an integral Number; the last is a Number, or a decimal digit string if precision options were applied.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _n_ be the number of elements in _multipliers_ + 1.
          1. Let _fractionDigits_ be _resolvedOpts_.[[FractionDigits]].
          1. Assert: _resolvedOpts_.[[SignificantDigits]] is *undefined*.
          1. If _fractionDigits_ is *undefined*, then
            1. If _value_ is *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub>, let _mv_ be ~negative-zero~; else let _mv_ be ℝ(_value_).
          1. Else,
            1. Let _formatter_ be CreateFormatterObject(_resolvedOpts_.[[RoundingMode]], _fractionDigits_, _fractionDigits_, *undefined*, *undefined*, *undefined*).
            1. Let _intlMV_ be ! ToIntlMathematicalValue(_value_).
            1. Let _rounded_ be FormatNumericToString(_formatter_, _intlMV_.[[Value]], 0, 0).
            1. Let _mv_ be _rounded_.[[RoundedNumber]].
          1. If _mv_ is ~negative-zero~, then
            1. Let _negative_ be *true*.
            1. Let _magnitude_ be 0.
          1. Else if _mv_ &lt; 0, then
            1. Let _negative_ be *true*.
            1. Let _magnitude_ be -_mv_.
          1. Else,
            1. Let _negative_ be *false*.
            1. Let _magnitude_ be _mv_.
          1. Let _parts_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _k_ be _n_ - 2.
          1. Repeat, while _k_ ≥ 0,
            1. Let _m_ be _multipliers_[_k_].
            1. Let _q_ be floor(_magnitude_ / _m_).
            1. Insert _magnitude_ - _q_ × _m_ as the first element of _parts_.
            1. Set _magnitude_ to _q_.
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ - 1.
          1. Insert _magnitude_ as the first element of _parts_.
          1. Let _entries_ be a new empty List.
          1. Set _k_ to 0.
          1. Repeat, while _k_ &lt; _n_,
            1. Let _part_ be _parts_[_k_].
            1. If _negative_ is *true* and _part_ = 0, let _signedPart_ be ~negative-zero~; else if _negative_ is *true*, let _signedPart_ be -_part_; else let _signedPart_ be _part_.
            1. If _k_ = _n_ - 1 and _fractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, append RoundToFractionDigits(_signedPart_, _fractionDigits_, _resolvedOpts_.[[RoundingMode]]) to _entries_.
            1. Else if _signedPart_ is ~negative-zero~, append *-0*<sub>𝔽</sub> to _entries_.
            1. Else, append 𝔽(_signedPart_) to _entries_.
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ + 1.
          1. Return _entries_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-the-amount-constructor">
    <h1>The Amount Constructor</h1>
    <p>The Amount constructor:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>is <dfn>%Amount%</dfn>.</li>
      <li>is the initial value of the the *"Amount"* property of the global object.</li>
      <li>creates and initializes a new Amount object when called as a constructor</li>
      <li>may be used as the value of an *extends* clause of a class definition.</li>
    </ul>
    <emu-clause id="sec-the-amount-constructor-value">
      <h1>Amount ( _value_ [ , _options_ ] )</h1>
      <emu-alg>
        1. If NewTarget is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. If _value_ is an Object, then
          1. Let _validatedOpts_ be ? GetAmountOptions(_options_).
          1. Let _unit_ be _validatedOpts_.[[Unit]].
          1. If _unit_ is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _components_ be GetUnitComponents(_unit_).
          1. Let _n_ be the number of elements in _components_.
          1. If _n_ = 1, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. If _validatedOpts_.[[SignificantDigits]] is not *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
          1. Let _rawEntries_ be ? ReadSequenceEntries(_value_, _components_).
          1. Let _entries_ be a new empty List.
          1. Let _k_ be 0.
          1. For each element _entry_ of _rawEntries_, do
            1. If _k_ = _n_ - 1, then
              1. Append RoundAmountValue(_entry_, _validatedOpts_) to _entries_.
            1. Else if _entry_ is an Intl MV Record, then
              1. If _entry_.[[Value]] is ~negative-zero~, append *"-0"* to _entries_.
              1. Else, append the String value consisting of the decimal representation of _entry_.[[Value]] with no leading zeros to _entries_.
            1. Else,
              1. Append _entry_ to _entries_.
            1. Set _k_ to _k_ + 1.
          1. Let _O_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%Amount.prototype%, « [[AmountValue]], [[Unit]] »).
          1. Set _O_.[[AmountValue]] to _entries_.
          1. Set _O_.[[Unit]] to _unit_.
          1. Return _O_.
        1. If _value_ is a String, then
          1. Let _parsed_ be ParseText(_value_, |StrFiniteNumericLiteral|).
          1. If _parsed_ is not a Parse Node, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Set _value_ to the StringIntlMV of _parsed_.
        1. Else if _value_ is not a Number or a BigInt, then
          1. Throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Let _validatedOpts_ be ? GetAmountOptions(_options_).
        1. Let _unit_ be _validatedOpts_.[[Unit]].
        1. If _unit_ is not *undefined* and the number of elements in GetUnitComponents(_unit_) is greater than 1, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Let _O_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%Amount.prototype%, « [[AmountValue]], [[Unit]] »).
        1. Set _O_.[[AmountValue]] to RoundAmountValue(_value_, _validatedOpts_).
        1. Set _O_.[[Unit]] to _unit_.
        1. Return _O_.
      </emu-alg>
      <emu-note>
        <p>When no precision options are given, Number and BigInt arguments are stored directly in [[AmountValue]], preserving the original type. String arguments and values resulting from precision options are stored as a String in canonical exponential notation, preserving the precision implied by the input.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A sequence unit requires an Object _value_: either an Array with one element per component, or an object with a property named after each component. Each entry is stored with its own type, except that a String entry in any position but the last is stored as a plain integer string. Precision options round only the last entry and never carry into an earlier one; balancing a sequence is the job of <emu-xref href="#sec-amount.prototype.convertto">convertTo</emu-xref>.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note>
        <p>A String argument must denote a finite mathematical value. *"NaN"*, *"Infinity"*, *"+Infinity"*, *"-Infinity"*, and *""* are rejected, as are Strings with leading or trailing white space or other extraneous contents.</p>
      </emu-note>
      <emu-note type="editor">
        <p>We intend to move ECMA-402 FormatNumericToString and its dependent operations to ECMA-262, possibly renamed.</p>
      </emu-note>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-amount-prototype-properties">
  <h1>Properties of the Amount Prototype</h1>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount.prototype.value">
    <h1>get Amount.prototype.value</h1>
    <p>This accessor property, whose set accessor function is *undefined*, returns the numeric value of the Amount, or a frozen Array of the values of a sequence-unit Amount. Its get accessor function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[AmountValue]]).
      1. Let _v_ be _O_.[[AmountValue]].
      1. If _v_ is a List, then
        1. Let _array_ be CreateArrayFromList(_v_).
        1. Perform ! SetIntegrityLevel(_array_, ~frozen~).
        1. Return _array_.
      1. Return _v_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>For a single-unit Amount the value may be a Number, BigInt, or String. It is a String in two cases: when the Amount was constructed from a String value, and when a finite value was rounded according to precision options, whether during construction or during unit conversion. The result of a unit conversion to which no precision options were applied is a Number.</p>
      <p>For a sequence-unit Amount the value is a new frozen Array on every access, with one element per component of the unit. Each element follows the rules above.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount.prototype.unit">
    <h1>get Amount.prototype.unit</h1>
    <p>This accessor property, whose set accessor function is *undefined*, returns a String value (or *undefined*) indicating the unit that this Amount has. Its get accessor function performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[AmountValue]]).
      1. Return _O_.[[Unit]].
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount.prototype.tostring">
    <h1>Amount.prototype.toString ( )</h1>
    <p>This method returns a String representation of the Amount, including a unit indicator in bracket notation.</p>
    <p>It performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[AmountValue]]).
      1. Let _v_ be _O_.[[AmountValue]].
      1. Let _u_ be _O_.[[Unit]].
      1. If _v_ is a List, return the string-concatenation of *"["*, RenderSequenceEntries(_v_, GetUnitComponents(_u_)), and *"]"*.
      1. Let _valueStr_ be RenderAmountValue(_v_).
      1. If _u_ is *undefined*, return the string-concatenation of *"["*, _valueStr_, and *" ~]"*.
      1. Return the string-concatenation of *"["*, _valueStr_, *" "*, _u_, and *"]"*.
    </emu-alg>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount.prototype.tolocalestring">
    <h1>Amount.prototype.toLocaleString ( [ _reserved1_ [ , _reserved2_ ] ] )</h1>
    <p>An ECMAScript implementation that includes the ECMA-402 Internationalization API must implement this method as specified in the ECMA-402 specification. If an ECMAScript implementation does not include the ECMA-402 API the following specification of this method is used:</p>
    <p>It performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[AmountValue]]).
      1. Return ? Call(%Amount.prototype.toString%, _O_, « »).
    </emu-alg>
    <p>The meanings of the optional parameters to this method are defined in the ECMA-402 specification; implementations that do not include ECMA-402 support must not use those parameter positions for anything else.</p>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-amount.prototype.convertto">
    <h1>Amount.prototype.convertTo ( _options_ )</h1>
    <p>This method returns a new Amount whose value is the result of converting this Amount’s value from its current unit to a target unit. The target unit is specified by _options_.</p>
    <p>It performs the following steps when called:</p>
    <emu-alg>
      1. Let _O_ be the *this* value.
      1. Perform ? RequireInternalSlot(_O_, [[AmountValue]]).
      1. If _O_.[[Unit]] is *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
      1. Let _source_ be ? GetAmountConversionSource(_O_).
      1. Let _opts_ be ? GetAmountOptions(_options_, _source_).
      1. Let _targetUnit_ be _opts_.[[Unit]].
      1. Let _targetComponents_ be GetUnitComponents(_targetUnit_).
      1. Let _result_ be OrdinaryObjectCreate(%Amount.prototype%, « [[AmountValue]], [[Unit]] »).
      1. If the number of elements in _targetComponents_ is 1, then
        1. Let _convertedValue_ be ? ConvertUnitValue(_source_.[[Value]], _source_.[[Unit]], _targetUnit_).
        1. Set _result_.[[AmountValue]] to RoundAmountValue(_convertedValue_, _opts_).
      1. Else,
        1. If _opts_.[[SignificantDigits]] is not *undefined*, throw a *TypeError* exception.
        1. Let _targetData_ be ? GetSequenceUnitData(_targetComponents_).
        1. Let _smallestUnit_ be the last element of _targetComponents_.
        1. Let _convertedValue_ be ? ConvertUnitValue(_source_.[[Value]], _source_.[[Unit]], _smallestUnit_).
        1. If _convertedValue_ is not finite, throw a *RangeError* exception.
        1. Set _result_.[[AmountValue]] to SplitIntoSequence(_convertedValue_, _targetData_.[[Multipliers]], _opts_).
      1. Set _result_.[[Unit]] to _targetUnit_.
      1. Return _result_.
    </emu-alg>
    <emu-note>
      <p>A sequence-unit source is first totalled in its smallest component unit, without creating an intermediate Amount. A sequence-unit target is produced by converting to its smallest component unit and then splitting. Converting a non-finite value to a sequence unit throws, because every entry but the last must be an integer.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-numbers-and-dates">
  <h1>Numbers and Dates</h1>
  <emu-clause id="sec-number-objects">
    <h1>Number Objects</h1>
    <emu-clause id="sec-properties-of-the-number-prototype-object">
      <h1>Properties of the Number Prototype Object</h1>
      <emu-clause id="sec-renderinexponentialnotation" type="abstract operation">
        <h1>RenderInExponentialNotation (
          _v_: a mathematical value or ~negative-zero~,
          _sigDigits_: a positive integer
        ): a String
        </h1>
        <dl class="header">
          <dt>description</dt>
          <dd>It returns _v_ rendered in canonical exponential notation with _sigDigits_ significant digits: an optional minus sign, a mantissa with one integer digit, an optional fractional part of length _sigDigits_ - 1, *"e"*, an explicit sign, and an unpadded base-10 exponent. The sign of negative zero is preserved.</dd>
        </dl>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _sign_ be the empty String.
          1. If _v_ is ~negative-zero~, then
            1. Set _sign_ to *"-"*.
            1. Set _v_ to 0.
          1. Else if _v_ &lt; 0, then
            1. Set _sign_ to *"-"*.
            1. Set _v_ to -_v_.
          1. If _v_ = 0, then
            1. Let _exponent_ be 0.
            1. Let _mantissaInt_ be 0.
          1. Else,
            1. Let _exponent_ be the unique integer such that 10<sup>_exponent_</sup> ≤ _v_ &lt; 10<sup>_exponent_ + 1</sup>.
            1. Let _mantissaInt_ be _v_ × 10<sup>_sigDigits_ - 1 - _exponent_</sup>.
          1. Assert: _mantissaInt_ is a non-negative integer with at most _sigDigits_ decimal digits.
          1. Let _intStr_ be the base-10 String representation of _mantissaInt_ with no leading zeros, or *"0"* if _mantissaInt_ is 0.
          1. Repeat, while the length of _intStr_ is less than _sigDigits_,
            1. Set _intStr_ to the string-concatenation of *"0"* and _intStr_.
          1. If _sigDigits_ = 1, then
            1. Let _mantissaStr_ be _intStr_.
          1. Else,
            1. Let _mantissaStr_ be the string-concatenation of the substring of _intStr_ from 0 to 1, *"."*, and the substring of _intStr_ from 1.
          1. If _exponent_ ≥ 0, let _expSign_ be *"+"*; else let _expSign_ be *"-"*.
          1. Let _expStr_ be the unique base-10 String representation of abs(_exponent_) with no leading zeros.
          1. Return the string-concatenation of _sign_, _mantissaStr_, *"e"*, _expSign_, and _expStr_.
        </emu-alg>
      </emu-clause>

      <emu-clause id="sec-number.prototype.toexponential" type="built-in function">
        <h1>Number.prototype.toExponential ( _fractionDigits_ )</h1>
        <p>This method returns a String containing this Number value represented in decimal exponential notation with one digit before the significand's decimal point and _fractionDigits_ digits after the significand's decimal point. If _fractionDigits_ is *undefined*, it includes as many significand digits as necessary to uniquely specify the Number (just like in ToString except that in this case the Number is always output in exponential notation).</p>
        <p>It performs the following steps when called:</p>
        <emu-alg>
          1. Let _number_ be ? ThisNumberValue(*this* value).
          1. Let _fractionCount_ be ? ToIntegerOrInfinity(_fractionDigits_).
          1. Assert: If _fractionDigits_ is *undefined*, then _fractionCount_ is 0.
          1. If _number_ is not finite, return Number::toString(_number_, 10).
          1. If _fractionCount_ &lt; 0 or _fractionCount_ > 100, throw a *RangeError* exception.
          1. Set _number_ to ℝ(_number_).
          1. Let _sign_ be the empty String.
          1. If _number_ &lt; 0, then
            1. Set _sign_ to *"-"*.
            1. Set _number_ to -_number_.
          1. If _number_ = 0, then
            1. <del>Let _significand_ be the String value consisting of _fractionCount_ + 1 occurrences of the code unit 0x0030 (DIGIT ZERO).</del>
            1. <ins>Let _intSignificand_ be 0.</ins>
            1. Let _exponent_ be 0.
          1. Else,
            1. If _fractionDigits_ is not *undefined*, then
              1. Let _exponent_ and _intSignificand_ be integers such that 10<sup>_fractionCount_</sup> ≤ _intSignificand_ &lt; 10<sup>_fractionCount_ + 1</sup> and for which _intSignificand_ × 10<sup>_exponent_ - _fractionCount_</sup> - _number_ is as close to zero as possible. If there are two such sets of _exponent_ and _intSignificand_, pick the _exponent_ and _intSignificand_ for which _intSignificand_ × 10<sup>_exponent_ - _fractionCount_</sup> is larger.
            1. Else,
              1. [id="step-number-proto-toexponential-intermediate-values"] Let _exponent_, _intSignificand_, and _ff_ be integers such that _ff_ ≥ 0, 10<sup>_ff_</sup> ≤ _intSignificand_ &lt; 10<sup>_ff_ + 1</sup>, 𝔽(_intSignificand_ × 10<sup>_exponent_ - _ff_</sup>) is 𝔽(_number_), and _ff_ is as small as possible. Note that the decimal representation of _intSignificand_ has _ff_ + 1 digits, _intSignificand_ is not divisible by 10, and the least significant digit of _intSignificand_ is not necessarily uniquely determined by these criteria.
              1. Set _fractionCount_ to _ff_.
            1. <del>Let _significand_ be the String value consisting of the digits of the decimal representation of _intSignificand_ (in order, with no leading zeroes).</del>
          1. <del>If _fractionCount_ ≠ 0, then</del>
            1. <del>Let _intPart_ be the first code unit of _significand_.</del>
            1. <del>Let _fractionalPart_ be the other _fractionCount_ code units of _significand_.</del>
            1. <del>Set _significand_ to the string-concatenation of _intPart_, *"."*, and _fractionalPart_.</del>
          1. <del>If _exponent_ = 0, then</del>
            1. <del>Let _exponentSign_ be *"+"*.</del>
            1. <del>Let _exponentDigits_ be *"0"*.</del>
          1. <del>Else,</del>
            1. <del>If _exponent_ > 0, then</del>
              1. <del>Let _exponentSign_ be *"+"*.</del>
            1. <del>Else,</del>
              1. <del>Assert: _exponent_ &lt; 0.</del>
              1. <del>Let _exponentSign_ be *"-"*.</del>
              1. <del>Set _exponent_ to -_exponent_.</del>
            1. <del>Let _exponentDigits_ be the String value consisting of the digits of the decimal representation of _exponent_ (in order, with no leading zeroes).</del>
          1. <del>Set _significand_ to the string-concatenation of _significand_, *"e"*, _exponentSign_, and _exponentDigits_.</del>
          1. <del>Return the string-concatenation of _sign_ and _significand_.</del>
          1. <ins>Return the string-concatenation of _sign_ and RenderInExponentialNotation(_intSignificand_ × 10<sup>_exponent_ - _fractionCount_</sup>, _fractionCount_ + 1).</ins>
        </emu-alg>
        <emu-note>
          <p>For implementations that provide more accurate conversions than required by the rules above, it is recommended that the following alternative version of step <emu-xref href="#step-number-proto-toexponential-intermediate-values"></emu-xref> be used as a guideline:</p>
          <emu-alg replaces-step="step-number-proto-toexponential-intermediate-values">
            1. Let _exponent_, _intSignificand_, and _ff_ be integers such that _ff_ ≥ 0, 10<sup>_ff_</sup> ≤ _intSignificand_ &lt; 10<sup>_ff_ + 1</sup>, 𝔽(_intSignificand_ × 10<sup>_exponent_ - _ff_</sup>) is 𝔽(_number_), and _ff_ is as small as possible. If there are multiple possibilities for _intSignificand_, choose _intSignificand_ such that 𝔽(_intSignificand_ × 10<sup>_exponent_ - _ff_</sup>) is closest to 𝔽(_number_). If there are two such possible values of _intSignificand_, choose the one that is even.
          </emu-alg>
        </emu-note>
      </emu-clause>
    </emu-clause>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-import href="./intl.emu"></emu-import>

<emu-clause id="sec-normative-references">
  <h1>Normative References</h1>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://unicode.org/reports/tr35/">Unicode Technical Standard #35: Unicode Locale Data Markup Language (LDML)</a>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://unicode.org/reports/tr35/tr35-general.html#Unit_Identifiers">Part 2 General, Unit Identifiers</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://unicode.org/reports/tr35/tr35-info.html#Unit_Conversion">Part 6 Supplemental, Unit Conversion</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://tc39.es/proposal-intl-sequence-units/">Intl Sequence Units (TC39 proposal)</a>, for the formatting of sequence units by ECMA-402
    </li>
  </ul>
</emu-clause>
